## §5 Findings

**F-1 — A8-as-written is falsified; "lossless" is an unmeetable exit criterion.**
SAEOS-1 §8 sets S1's exit at *"round-trip is lossless."* Measured loss is 0.236 and cannot
reach 0 against sealed history, because the missing text was never authored. Shipping S1
against an unreachable criterion guarantees a false NO-GO at the S1 gate. **§8's S1 row must
be amended before S1 is built** (see C1).

**F-2 — The loss is source-side, not extractor-side.** Not one `absent` cell was absent
because the artifact was *unstructured*. Every one was absent because the *field was never
written*. This distinguishes A8's two readings: "PAEOS emits prose, not typed bundles"
(**true, and irrelevant** — the CER-4 form parses reliably) versus "PAEOS does not record the
information" (**true for 9.7% of fields, and decisive**). A better extractor recovers nothing.
A better *template* recovers most of it.

**F-3 — Extraction fidelity tracks ceremony depth, and the corpus is bimodal.** B0.0 (0.361)
vs B0.1/B0.2 (0.176/0.175). The discriminator is the CER-4 six-question close-out plus a real
adversary pass. The predictive claim: **any goal that completes L17 to protocol will extract
at ≈0.18; any goal that does not will extract at ≈0.36.**

**F-4 — `tradeoff_precedents` is the one method at genuine risk, and `axis` is why.**
SAEOS-1 §8 makes S2 (`tradeoff_precedents` only) *"the whole bet."* Its query key is `axes`.
Across three goals: one goal has **zero** TradeOff nodes; the other two have one each, both
with `axis` **partial** — an axis inferable by a reader, absent as a field. A projection
method cannot match on an inferred key without SAKG reasoning, which §5.2 forbids
(*"defined as a traversal, so SAKG never reasons (Δ5)"*). **S2 as specified is not reachable
from the corpus as emitted.** This is the probe's sharpest result and it is a *prerequisite*,
not a nice-to-have (see C2).

**F-5 — The Ω2 outcome loop is real, and it is the strongest positive.** SAEOS-1 §6.1b rule 3
calls outcomes *"the loop that makes SAKG memory rather than archive."* All three goals yield
an `Outcome` with verdict + window + subject extractable from history that already happened:
B0.0's deferred spike ran GREEN; B0.1's DEBT-0003 was discharged by SQLite; B0.2 *caused* a
founder-ratified scope reduction of another goal's debt. A one-week-old corpus already
contains real consequential edges. Nothing in A8 predicted this, and it is the single best
argument for GO.

**F-6 — `Scar` round-trips losslessly, as §4.3 claims.** B0.2's temp-file enumeration defect
yields root cause, detection signature and guard action at 3/3, all quotable. The Ω1 scar
claim is validated on n=1.

**F-7 (NEW ADVERSARIAL FINDING — not recorded in SAEOS-1 §9) — §6.1b's emission diagram is
invalid on the Trace-A path.** §6.1b sources `Option` from L04 candidates, `TradeOff` from the
L06 matrix, `Constraint` from the L11 contract and `Component` from the L08 artifact set. But
`operations/ENGINEERING_LIFECYCLE.md` §"Router compatibility" states that for **Trace-A**
(reversible, cheaply verified — *by design the majority path*) the Runtime
**auto-discharges L03–L11 in one pass, recording a machine "auto-pass, Trace-A" evidence
stub.** A stub is not a matrix. **On Trace-A, four of §6.1b's six named sources do not
exist.** SAEOS-1 nowhere acknowledges ceremony depth. This is a direct contradiction between
the ratified architecture and the ratified lifecycle, and it means the emission contract must
be specified **per trace depth** or Trace-A goals will emit near-empty bundles that
nonetheless pass a naïve schema validator. *(The three probed goals predate ceremony-depth
classification, so their thinness has an independent second cause — but the structural defect
is about all future emission, not about them.)*

**F-8 — `reviews/tasks/*_review.md` is the wrong extraction target.** The 10-point protocol
form contributed essentially nothing beyond `status`. If S1 is built against
`reviews/tasks/`, it will measure a loss ratio far worse than 0.236 and produce a spurious
NO-GO. The extraction target is the **CER-4 six-question close-out** (`docs/*-constitutional-review.md`)
joined to `ledger/debt/` and the Atlas row.

### Spec gaps surfaced (SAKG-side, block `F-ENVELOPE`)

**GAP-1 — `confidence` is "derived, never authored" with no derivation specified.** §4.2
mandates derivation and TMS propagation along `derived_from`/`supports`, but SAEOS-1 defines
no function, no inputs, no range semantics. A round-trip test cannot be written for a field
whose value is undefined. **`F-ENVELOPE` must not freeze the schema until this is specified**
— freezing is irreversible per the router matrix.

**GAP-2 — `coordinates` has no dimension registry.** §4.2 requires
`coordinates: {dimension: [value]}` (✓8). Values are plentiful (trust zone, phase, clause,
slice); dimension *names* exist nowhere. An extractor that invents them creates architecture
outside L08 — barred by `ENGINEERING_LIFECYCLE.md` global rule 3 and CER-6.

**GAP-3 — `Option` has no `deferred` state.** B0.1 deferred Postgres; it did not reject it.
Casting `deferred` to `rejected` writes a falsehood into the corpus, and §4.3 calls rejected
options *"the corpus's scarcest, most valuable knowledge"* — so the cast corrupts precisely
the highest-value data. Needs an `Option.disposition` enum, not a boolean.

